WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.719
Authentication answers the question,

2
00:00:03.799 --> 00:00:08.871
who is making this request or using this
application session.

3
00:00:08.951 --> 00:00:13.828
Blazor relies on ASP.NET Core
authentication mechanisms,

4
00:00:13.908 --> 00:00:20.444
but the details depend on hosting model,
identity provider, browser behavior,

5
00:00:20.524 --> 00:00:27.059
and whether the application is calling
protected APIs from server-side code or

6
00:00:27.139 --> 00:00:29.285
browser-delivered code.

7
00:00:29.365 --> 00:00:35.706
Authentication state lets components
understand the current user context.

8
00:00:35.786 --> 00:00:40.858
A component may use that state to show a
signed-in experience,

9
00:00:40.938 --> 00:00:45.328
display the user's name, choose navigation
options,

10
00:00:45.408 --> 00:00:49.407
or conditionally render parts of the
interface.

11
00:00:49.487 --> 00:00:52.413
That is useful for user experience,

12
00:00:52.493 --> 00:00:58.541
but it is not the same as enforcing access
to protected data or operations.

13
00:00:58.621 --> 00:01:05.644
Showing or hiding UI based on
authentication state should be treated as presentation

14
00:01:05.724 --> 00:01:06.309
logic.

15
00:01:06.389 --> 00:01:11.754
A hidden menu item does not protect the
server resource behind it.

16
00:01:11.834 --> 00:01:18.272
A component that does not render a button
does not prove that the corresponding

17
00:01:18.352 --> 00:01:20.693
operation is inaccessible.

18
00:01:20.773 --> 00:01:27.601
Protected data and state changes must
still be enforced by server-side handlers or

19
00:01:27.681 --> 00:01:28.169
APIs.

20
00:01:28.249 --> 00:01:35.272
Cookie-based and token-based designs have
different browser and API implications.

21
00:01:35.352 --> 00:01:40.230
Cookie-based browser flows may depend on
cookie settings,

22
00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:45.870
same-site behavior, antiforgery protection
for unsafe requests,

23
00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:49.266
and server-side session expectations.

24
00:01:49.346 --> 00:01:55.394
Token-based API flows require careful
design around issuance, storage,

25
00:01:55.474 --> 00:01:59.278
audience, expiration, and browser
exposure.

26
00:01:59.358 --> 00:02:04.821
Authentication failure behavior should be
predictable and safe.

27
00:02:04.901 --> 00:02:08.217
A browser page, interactive component,

28
00:02:08.297 --> 00:02:12.199
and API client may expect different
responses.

29
00:02:12.279 --> 00:02:19.497
The application should avoid revealing
sensitive implementation details while still

30
00:02:19.577 --> 00:02:26.405
giving users and operators enough
information to understand that identity was not

31
00:02:26.485 --> 00:02:29.411
established or was no longer valid.

32
00:02:29.491 --> 00:02:34.759
The secure baseline is to document how
identity is established,

33
00:02:34.839 --> 00:02:38.741
how components receive authentication
state,

34
00:02:38.821 --> 00:02:43.015
what APIs or server handlers rely on that
identity,

35
00:02:43.095 --> 00:02:48.948
and how production behavior differs from
local development behavior.

36
00:02:49.028 --> 00:02:54.100
Identity assumptions should not live only
in component code.
